# A short story for the Aside engine.
# Third register. Night Shift is a demo, Overtime is horror and performance, The Lamp Room is warm and about
# tending. This one is quiet, wry, and slightly haunted: a night auditor reconciling a day that does not balance,
# and the thing in the ledger that should not be there. Six scenes, three endings, one sitting.

title: The Discrepancy
author: Roxanne
start: desk

== desk ==
bg office_night
music sea_swell
The night audit is the same every night, which is the point of it.
Room 101 paid in full. Room 104 left early and was not charged. Room 112 is still in there and will be until
Thursday, and has asked twice for a pot that does not exist.

You are halfway through the folio when you find the line that does not belong.

* Look at it properly. -> read
* Skip it. It is not your job to be curious. -> skip

== skip ==
You move the cursor down and finish the sheet. The totals balance. They always balance.
The line is still there in the morning, because lines do not get tired.
-> morning

== read ==
sfx clank
ROOM 000. CHECK-IN: YESTERDAY. CHECK-OUT: —. RATE: 0.00. NAME: (unreadable).

There is no room 000. You have worked here eleven years and you would know.
The rate is zero and the stay is open, which is how the system records a room that is occupied by nothing
and has been for a long time.

* Ask the system to explain it. -> ask_system
* Open the room's notes. -> notes
* Print the line and put it in your pocket. -> print

== ask_system ==
~ asked true
SYSTEM: Room 000 is not in the property management plan.
SYSTEM: Room 000 was not added by any user.
SYSTEM: Room 000 has one note.
-> notes

== notes ==
~ read_note true
The note is one line long and it is in your handwriting.

"Check on her at 3."

You do not remember writing it. You remember writing it every night for eleven years.
-> three_am

== print ==
The printer takes the sheet and gives it back warm, and you fold it twice and put it in your coat,
which is what you do with things you intend to think about later.
-> three_am

== three_am ==
bg cove
music music_box
At three the building is at its quietest, which is not the same as its emptiest.
You stand outside 000 for a while, which is to say you stand at a wall, because 000 is a wall.

* Knock. -> knock
* Put your hand flat on it and wait. -> wait
* Go back to the desk and finish the audit. -> back

== knock ==
sfx door_open
You knock. It is a wall. You knock anyway, which is the sort of thing you would have to explain to anyone
watching, and nobody is.
A long pause. Then, from the other side of the wall, a voice you have heard before and cannot place:
keeper: You are late tonight.
~ heard true
-> answer

== wait ==
You put your hand flat on the wall and wait. The building does what it does at three in the morning, which is
mostly nothing, at length.
After eleven minutes you feel it: not a knock, just warmth, the way a kettle is warm before it is hot.
keeper: You are late tonight.
~ heard true
-> answer

== back ==
~ left true
You go back to the desk and finish the audit. The totals balance. They always balance.
In the morning the line for room 000 is still there, and you have worked here eleven years, and you will be
here eleven more.
-> morning

== answer ==
keeper: I keep the other shift.
keeper: Somebody has to be awake in this building at three, and it turns out you have been doing it
for eleven years, and so have I, and neither of us has ever said hello.

* Say hello. -> hello [if heard]
* Ask who she is. -> who
* Say nothing. -> quiet_end

== who ==
keeper: Nobody in the file. That is the whole trick of it.
keeper: A rate of zero and a stay that never closes. You can be here forever if nobody is paying.
~ knows true
-> hello

== hello ==
keeper: There. That was not difficult.
keeper: Same time tomorrow.
~ greeted true
-> morning

== quiet_end ==
bg office_dawn
music sea_swell
You say nothing, and she waits, and the waiting is not unkind, and then it is three-forty and you are
standing at a wall with your hand on it and no reason you could give anyone.
-> morning

== morning ==
bg office_dawn
music sea_swell
The morning shift arrives at six and finds the audit finished and the desk tidy, which is how they find it
every day, and they do not ask how.

* Look for the line for room 000. -> found [if greeted]
* Look for it anyway, the way you do every morning. -> found_quiet [if left]
* Ask about room 000. -> asking [if asked]
* Check whether the note is still in your handwriting. -> note_again [if read_note]
* Ask who she is. -> who_she_is [if knows]
* Go home. -> home

== found ==
The line is there. It has a check-out time now, at 3:47, and a rate of 0.00, and a name.

The name is yours, which is not the answer you were hoping for and is, on reflection, the only one that fits.
-> home

== found_quiet ==
The line is there, and there is a second line under it, in the same unreadable hand.

It says: "She came back. She always comes back."
-> home

== who_she_is ==
The morning manager does not know. The file does not know. The wall, if you asked it, might.

You do not ask the wall. You have eleven years of practice at not asking the wall.
-> home

== note_again ==
The note is still there. It is still your handwriting.

Under it, in the same hand, is a second line you did not write and did not not-write:
"Same time tomorrow."
-> home

== asking ==
The morning manager looks at you for a moment longer than the question deserves.
manager: There is no room 000. There has never been a room 000. Go home, you look like you have been up.
-> home

== home ==
bg office_dawn
You walk home in the grey part of the morning, and the sea is on your left because the sea is always on your
left, and it sounds the way it sounded at three.
-> END
